
[38;5;231m⏺[39m This one is a one-line edit, but it lands on session lifetime, so I want your explicit go-ahead before making it.

  [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m is [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m, and [38;5;153mserver.js:3[39m confirms it's the actual session expiry. Going to 480
  means a stolen or abandoned session stays valid for a full working day instead of half an hour — an unattended laptop,
  a leaked session cookie, or a token lifted from a shared machine all keep working roughly 16x longer, and revocation
  only happens at the next login.

  If that's the intent (long-lived internal tool, or there's an idle-timeout/re-auth layer elsewhere that bounds real
  exposure), say go and I'll set it to 480.

  Two alternatives if the goal is just "stop logging people out mid-work":
  - Keep a short absolute/idle timeout and add sliding renewal on activity — active users never get bounced, idle
    sessions still die fast.
  - A middle value (2–4 hours) covers most work sessions with far less exposure.

  Tell me which and I'll make the change.

